id: analyzed
name: Analyzed
kind: conditions
maxStacks: 1
# Not a Combat Condition: the entry says "they become Analyzed" and never calls it one.
combatCondition: no
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Somebody has your measure. Whoever Analyzed you rolls better against you until the
  end of their next turn.
text: >
  Analyzed: the Analysis Maneuver's mark. "Target an Opponent. They become Analyzed
  until the end of your next turn."

---

# Not a Combat Condition the rulebook lists, and the entry that creates it does not
# call it one: "they become Analyzed", where Called Shot's examples say "gains the
# Impediment Combat Condition". It is kept here because this is where a mark on a
# character with a clock on it lives, and building a third family for one mark would
# be worse than borrowing the right one.
#
# No effects of its own, which is the other half of why it fits here. What being
# Analyzed costs you is written in the Analysis Maneuver, because the bonus belongs to
# whoever Analyzed you rather than to everybody: "increase YOUR Combat Rolls against
# Analyzed Opponents". A passive here would hand it to the whole table.
#
# Who Analyzed you is the clock. The mark sits on you and the clock that takes it off
# sits on them - "until the end of YOUR next turn" is their turn - so the entry on
# their character naming you is both the timer and the record of who it was.
